  "I5" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given specific case of COLD1 and general pattern of pathways, therefore diverse mechanisms known."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper proposes that naturally occurring 57-bp and 284-bp deletion alleles of the wild-rice-derived CTB3 gene, encoding a calmodulin-binding transcription factor in japonica rice, enhance cold tolerance at the booting stage by reducing OsTCP19-mediated repression, activating OsTPP1 to lower Tre6P, promoting sugar-transporter gene activity and panicle sugar accumulation, and thereby provide an adaptive genetic resource for cold-resilient rice breeding.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A booting-stage cold-tolerance breeding proposal is supported when a yield-relevant knowledge gap is paired with a mechanistically characterized adaptive allele that improves cold tolerance."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Because cold injury at the booting stage decreases yield and fewer booting-stage cold-tolerance genes are known, identifying genes for this stage is agronomically relevant."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) The CTB3 findings jointly indicate a wild-rice-derived adaptive allele whose regulation and OsTPP1/Tre6P-mediated sugar-accumulation mechanism enhance cold tolerance."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Specific reported booting-stage cold-tolerance genes such as bZIP73 exist, while the paper states that fewer genes for cold tolerance at the booting stage have been identified."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) The contrast between many identified seedling-stage cold-response genes and fewer identified booting-stage cold-tolerance genes motivates a booting-stage-focused gene-discovery gap."];
